<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Alien Act 1705</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1705">1705</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Alien Act</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Alien Act 1705 was a law of the English Parliament designed to force <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a> into negotiations for union. It threatened that Scots in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> would be treated as foreigners and that the main Scottish exports to England would be banned unless Scotland accepted the Hanoverian succession or began talks. Scotland agreed to negotiate, and the threatening clauses were repealed before the Union of 1707.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/alien-act-1705.jpg" alt="Alien Act 1705" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sodacan · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coat_of_arms_of_England_(1702%E2%80%931707).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The Alien Act 1705 (3 &amp; 4 Ann. c. 6) was passed by the Parliament of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> in February 1705 in response to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parliament-of-scotland">Parliament of Scotland</a>'s Act of Security 1704, which was itself partly a response to the English Act of Settlement 1701. Lord Godolphin, the Lord High Treasurer, was instrumental in the Union of 1707 and in all the acts leading up to it. The stated purpose of the act was to prevent the inconveniences that would arise if the two kingdoms did not become one union.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Terms</h2><p>Scottish nationals in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> were to be treated as aliens, and estates held by Scots as alien property, which made inheritance much less certain. The act placed an embargo on the import of Scottish products into England and the English colonies, about half of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>'s trade, covering goods such as linen, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coal">coal</a>. It also banned the export of arms, ammunition and horses to Scotland, so that the Scots could not raise an army and invade England.</p><p>The act was to be suspended if the Scots entered into negotiations for a union of the parliaments of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>. It required them to settle the succession or to authorise union negotiations by 25 December 1705.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The Scots insisted that the act be repealed before they would enter treaty negotiations. In late December news reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a> that both the Commons and the Lords had agreed to repeal it. Combined with English financial offers to refund Scottish losses on the Darien scheme, the act achieved its aim, leading to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acts-of-union-1707">Acts of Union 1707</a>, which united the two countries as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-great-britain">Kingdom of Great Britain</a>. The whole act was formally repealed by section 1 of, and the schedule to, the Statute Law Revision Act 1867, which came into force on 15 July 1867.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Alien Act 1705” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>England replied with the <b>Alien Act</b> of 1705, threatening to treat Scots as foreigners and ban Scottish imports unless Scotland negotiated.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-great-britain">Kingdom of Great Britain · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
